&lt;li&gt;National Institute of Diabetes and Digestive and Kidney Diseases. Gallstones. &lt;a href=&quot;https://www.niddk.nih.gov/health-information/digestive-diseases/gallstones&quot;&gt;https://www.niddk.nih.gov/health-information/digestive-diseases/gallstones&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Fort JM et al. Bowel habit after cholecystectomy. Gastroenterology. &lt;a href=&quot;https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/8795637/&quot;&gt;https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/8795637/&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;American College of Surgeons. Cholecystectomy Surgical Patient Education. &lt;a href=&quot;https://www.facs.org/education/patient-education&quot;&gt;https://www.facs.org/education/patient-education&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ol&gt;
&lt;hr&gt;
&lt;h2&gt;Understanding Fat Digestion Changes After Gallbladder Removal&lt;/h2&gt;
&lt;p&gt;The gallbladder serves as a reservoir for bile, storing and concentrating it between meals. When we eat fatty foods, the gallbladder contracts, releasing concentrated bile into the small intestine to emulsify and digest fats.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;After cholecystectomy, bile flows continuously from the liver directly into the intestine without storage or concentration. This fundamental change has several implications:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Reduced bile concentration&lt;/strong&gt;: The body can no longer deliver a large bolus of concentrated bile during meals&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Continuous low-flow state&lt;/strong&gt;: Bile volume during eating may be insufficient to process large fat loads&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Disrupted coordination&lt;/strong&gt;: The synchronized release of bile with food intake is temporarily disturbed&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;h2&gt;Common Symptoms of Fat Malabsorption&lt;/h2&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Approximately 15-30% of patients experience some degree of fat digestion issues after surgery:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;h3&gt;Steatorrhea (Fatty Stools)&lt;/h3&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Oily, floating, foul-smelling stools indicate undigested fat passing through the digestive tract.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;h3&gt;Postprandial D
